      "math_academy_id": "q-74587",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219315/assets/9a1f9f1877ae0ecbc37464c5b8b20821070e7927e59dea8d930ceb8f37313722.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219315/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a parallelogram since it has $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since the opposite sides of a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. It has no pairs of opposite sides that are parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shape $S$ is not parallelogram.",
